    "explanationEn": "Fatigue is characterised by exactly the combination in the stem: stresses well below the tensile strength, a period in service, then SUDDEN fracture. Microcracks initiate at stress concentrations — notches, holes, machining marks — and each stress cycle advances the crack a minute amount until, after tens of thousands to millions of cycles, the remaining section cannot carry the load and snaps. The fracture surface typically shows a smooth region of slow crack growth beside a rough final-fracture zone. Using generous radii instead of sharp corners and avoiding surface scratches reduces stress concentration for this reason. Plastic deformation gives warning through visible elongation; corrosion shows visible rust; and overload means a single excursion above the strength, contradicting \"well below the tensile strength\"."
